          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have asthma and I use a blue inhaler.",
          "answer": "Thanks for sharing. You haven't described any emergency, so I'll keep this general.\n\nI searched the emergency guidance and found one relevant passage. The \"Severe difficulty breathing\" guidance (an educational summary from emergency medicine and respiratory society sources) lists asthma attack among the causes of acute, severe breathlessness. It says that someone with a prescribed reliever inhaler should use it according to their own action plan, and that the person should be kept sitting upright while help is on the way. It also says that how someone is breathing and whether they can speak tell more than a fingertip reading does.\n\nMy search did not find guidance on how often to use a blue inhaler, when to use it, or when asthma symptoms mean you need to see a clinician. Your asthma action plan, from your own clinician, is the best source for those questions.\n\nIf your breathing suddenly gets much harder, or you struggle to speak, seek emergency help straight away. Don't wait to see whether it improves.\n\nIf you tell me what you'd like to understand, such as how asthma inhalers work or what to ask at your next appointment, I can search for that.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
