          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Should I be on something else as well?",
          "answer": "The guidance I found doesn't say whether you need another medicine. It describes a \"stepwise approach to controller therapy\" in the document \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\" (global respiratory strategy and national asthma panel guidance). But the passage I could retrieve covers only part of that document. It doesn't say when a controller is added to a reliever, so I can't answer that from it.\n\nThe passage did cover a few related points:\n\n- **Experts differ on the reliever itself.** Global strategy guidance prefers a combined inhaler that includes a steroid and a fast-acting bronchodilator as the reliever. Some national panel guidance says a short-acting reliever with a regular controller remains appropriate in mild asthma.\n- **Technique comes first.** The document warns that adding medicine to fix poor inhaler technique \"treats the wrong problem.\"\n- **Reviews are regular.** It recommends a review one to three months after starting or changing treatment, then every three to twelve months. It also recommends a review within one week after a flare-up.\n\nWhether you need a second medicine depends on your symptoms, how often you use your blue inhaler, and your asthma history. Your clinician or asthma nurse can assess that. Useful questions to bring:\n\n- \"Is my reliever use a sign my asthma isn't well controlled?\"\n- \"Is my inhaler technique correct?\"\n- \"Should I have a regular controller, and when would we review it?\"\n\nIf your breathing gets suddenly worse, or you struggle to speak, seek emergency help straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
